# Copy to .env and edit. Variables left empty use the default shown in the comment.
# Most settings can also be saved from the console (docs/console.md); a variable set here wins.

# Postgres connection URL (docker compose sets it for you).
OHS_DATABASE_URL=postgres://ohs:ohs@localhost:5432/ohs?sslmode=disable
# 32 random bytes, base64. Run `ohs doctor` once to get a fresh one. Keep it safe and back it up apart from the database:
# losing it loses stored OAuth tokens and, while the vault root key is derived from it (the default), every health record.
OHS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Instead of OHS_ENCRYPTION_KEY (not both): a file holding the key. If the file is missing, a random key is
# generated into it (mode 0600) on first start. Back the file up with the database.
# OHS_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=/var/lib/ohs/secrets/encryption-key
# Only while rotating the key (docs/key-rotation.md): the keys OHS_ENCRYPTION_KEY replaced, comma separated. They still open, never seal.
# Remove one only after `ohs admin rekey` passed: it also moves the vault to the root key derived from the new key.
# OHS_PREVIOUS_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=
# Health data vault: every health value is sealed under a key of its user, all of them under one root key.
# Key provider that holds the root key: local (default), hashicorp, azurekv or awskms. `ohs doctor` checks it opens.
# OHS_VAULT_KEY_PROVIDER=local
# local: a 32 byte base64 root key (or a file holding it) instead of the one derived from OHS_ENCRYPTION_KEY.
# OHS_VAULT_ROOT_KEY=
# OHS_VAULT_ROOT_KEY_FILE=/var/lib/ohs/secrets/vault-root-key
# Only while rotating the root key (`ohs vault rotate root`): the keys it replaced, comma separated. They still open, never wrap.
# OHS_VAULT_ROOT_KEY_PREVIOUS=
# hashicorp: transit key created with derived=true. The token comes from VAULT_TOKEN or the token file.
# OHS_VAULT_HASHICORP_ADDR=https://vault.example.org:8200
# OHS_VAULT_HASHICORP_MOUNT=transit
# OHS_VAULT_HASHICORP_KEY=ohs
# OHS_VAULT_HASHICORP_TOKEN_FILE=/run/secrets/vault-token
# azurekv and awskms: the key. Both are in the published image and the release binaries (a build from source needs
# `-tags azurekv,awskms`). Credentials: managed identity or the standard AZURE_* variables; the standard AWS chain.
# OHS_VAULT_AZURE_KEY_ID=
# OHS_VAULT_AWS_KEY_ARN=
# Unwrapped user keys kept in memory: how many users and for how long (defaults 1024 and 10m).
# OHS_VAULT_CACHE_USERS=1024
# OHS_VAULT_CACHE_TTL=10m
# Public base URL of this server, used for OAuth callbacks (default http://localhost:8080).
OHS_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8080
# Where raw files go: file:///abs/path or s3://bucket?endpoint=&region=&path_style=true (default file:///var/lib/ohs/raw).
OHS_RAW_STORE=file:///var/lib/ohs/raw
# Roles this process runs, comma separated: api, collect, etl (default api,collect,etl).
OHS_ROLES=api,collect,etl
# Name of a tenant created at first start so you can issue an API key right away (empty = none).
OHS_BOOTSTRAP_TENANT=
# File that receives that first key, mode 0600 (default: bootstrap-api-key next to the raw store directory).
# OHS_BOOTSTRAP_KEY_FILE=
# Listen address (default :8080).
OHS_LISTEN=:8080
# Log level: debug, info, warn or error (default info).
OHS_LOG_LEVEL=info
# Expose Prometheus metrics on /metrics: true or false (default true).
OHS_METRICS=true
# The admin console, a web page on /console to turn providers on and save their keys, create users and
# send them a connection link, start a sync, manage webhooks and API keys, and read the audit log.
# Off by default. To turn it on, set OHS_CONSOLE=true, restart, open http://localhost:8080/console
# and paste a tenant API key (ohs_sk_...). Values: true, read-only (same pages, no action) or
# false (default: /console answers 404). See docs/console.md.
OHS_CONSOLE=false
# First run from a browser: with OHS_CONSOLE on, /console/setup creates the first tenant and shows its API key
# once, for whoever types this token (16+ characters, openssl rand -hex 32). Closed for good once any tenant exists.
# See docs/setup.md. Empty = off.
# OHS_SETUP_TOKEN=
# Instead of OHS_SETUP_TOKEN (not both): a file holding the token, generated (openssl rand -hex 32 style)
# into it on first start when missing.
# OHS_SETUP_TOKEN_FILE=/var/lib/ohs/secrets/setup-token
# Bearer token Prometheus sends to scrape /metrics (16+ characters, openssl rand -hex 32). Without it, or OHS_METRICS_LISTEN, /metrics refuses every scrape.
# OHS_METRICS_TOKEN=
# Serve /metrics on a private address of its own instead of the API listener, for example 127.0.0.1:9464.
# OHS_METRICS_LISTEN=
# /healthz, /readyz and /metrics of a process without the api role (OHS_ROLES=collect or etl), without a credential:
# 127.0.0.1:8081 by default there, off with the api role. off turns it off. See docs/observability.md.
# OHS_OPS_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8081
# OpenTelemetry traces (optional, off by default; see docs/observability.md). Setting an OTLP
# endpoint turns them on; the other standard OTEL_ variables are honoured too.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=ohs
# Delete raw files AND normalized data (samples, sleep, workouts, daily summaries) older than this many days, 0 keeps everything forever (default 0).
OHS_RETENTION_DAYS=0
# Store nothing from a source until the user's consent for it is recorded (default false: no consent recorded stores everything).
# OHS_CONSENT_REQUIRED=false
# Delete audit events older than this many days, once a day; 0 keeps every event (default 365).
OHS_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# Device uploads (Apple Health) one api process handles at once; more answer 503 with Retry-After (default 8).
OHS_INGEST_CONCURRENCY=8
# Of those uploads, the share one tenant is sure to get: past it a tenant takes a slot only while others keep a small
# reserve free (default half of OHS_INGEST_CONCURRENCY, rounded up).
# OHS_INGEST_TENANT_CONCURRENCY=4
# How far one uploaded part may expand, in MiB (default 64, at most 512).
# OHS_INGEST_MAX_DECOMPRESSED_MIB=64
# HTTP connections the api role holds open at once; one over it is closed at once (default 4096, about
# 35 KB each). A request must be read within 2 minutes (5 for an upload part).
# OHS_MAX_CONNECTIONS=4096
# Pulls of one provider running at once, across every collect instance (default 4), and how many of them may be
# backfills of new connections (default 2). See docs/background-sync.md#provider-rate-limits.
# OHS_PULLS_PER_PROVIDER=4
# OHS_BACKFILLS_PER_PROVIDER=2
# Pulls one collect process runs at once (default 4, 1 to 256), and pulls one scheduling pass queues, each
# minute (default 1000, 1 to 100000). See docs/background-sync.md#scheduling-at-scale.
# OHS_PULL_CONCURRENCY=4
# OHS_SCHEDULE_BATCH=1000
# How far back a new connection is pulled, in days, for every provider (1 to 1825, five years). Unset, each
# provider keeps its own window, 30 days for most. See docs/background-sync.md#backfill.
# OHS_BACKFILL_DAYS=
# Processing jobs each tier of one etl process runs at once (default 4, 1 to 64; there are two tiers), and how
# many of them may belong to one user, across every etl process (default 1, 0 for no cap).
# OHS_ETL_CONCURRENCY=4
# OHS_ETL_JOBS_PER_USER=1
# Delete sync log rows and finished processing jobs older than this many days, once a day (default 90; 0
# means the default, 1 to 13 count as 14, -1 keeps them for ever).
# OHS_TRACKING_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# Let outgoing webhooks use plain http and reach private or loopback addresses: true or false (default false).
# Keep it false on a server that faces the internet: it turns off the SSRF guard.
# OHS_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE=false
# Webhook deliveries one etl process posts at once (default 8, 1 to 256), how many of them go to one
# endpoint, across every etl process (default 2, at most OHS_WEBHOOK_CONCURRENCY), and how many to all
# the endpoints of one tenant (default half of OHS_WEBHOOK_CONCURRENCY, at least 1, at most
# OHS_WEBHOOK_CONCURRENCY; an instance with a single tenant can set it to OHS_WEBHOOK_CONCURRENCY).
# OHS_WEBHOOK_CONCURRENCY=8
# OHS_WEBHOOK_PER_ENDPOINT=2
# OHS_WEBHOOK_PER_TENANT=4
# Failed attempts in a row after which a webhook endpoint is paused (default 5, 0 never pauses).
# OHS_WEBHOOK_PAUSE_AFTER=5
# Hours the secret a webhook rotation replaced keeps signing next to the new one (default 24, 0 to 168;
# 0 ends it at once).
# OHS_WEBHOOK_ROTATION_GRACE_HOURS=24
# Event streams (GET /v1/events/stream) one api process holds open at once (default 1000, 0 turns
# the event stream off), and those of one tenant (default 10, at most OHS_STREAM_MAX).
# OHS_STREAM_MAX=1000
# OHS_STREAM_MAX_PER_TENANT=10
# Rate limits of the provider-facing routes, "rate/burst" in requests per second, 0 disables one
# (see docs/self-hosting.md#rate-limits). Notifications per client address (default 30/120),
# notifications per provider (default 300/600), OAuth callbacks and first-run setup tries per client address (default 2/20).
# OHS_RATE_LIMIT_INBOUND=30/120
# OHS_RATE_LIMIT_INBOUND_PROVIDER=300/600
# OHS_RATE_LIMIT_OAUTH=2/20
# Rate limits of the authenticated API, same format: requests of one credential, an API key or the tokens
# of one user (default 200/2000), and refused keys or tokens per client address (default 1/60): past it
# the refused ones of that address answer 429 instead of 401, valid ones are still served. Behind a
# reverse proxy this needs OHS_TRUSTED_PROXIES, or every client counts as the proxy's address.
# OHS_RATE_LIMIT_API=200/2000
# OHS_RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_FAILURES=1/60
# Gzip JSON, NDJSON, FHIR, CSV and YAML responses for clients that accept it: true or false (default true).
# Set false behind a reverse proxy that compresses.
# OHS_COMPRESSION=true
# Reverse proxies whose X-Forwarded-For names the client address, comma-separated CIDRs (default none).
# Set it behind a reverse proxy: the rate limits per client address count the proxy's address otherwise.
# OHS_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.16.0.0/12
# Image tag docker compose runs (optional, default 0.3.0; pass --env-file .env to compose to use it).
# OHS_VERSION=0.3.0
# Compose only, not read by ohs: host port the API is published on (optional, default 8080;
# pass --env-file .env to compose to use it). Set OHS_PUBLIC_URL to the same port.
# OHS_HTTP_PORT=8080
# Compose only: host address the API port listens on (optional, default 127.0.0.1, this machine only).
# 0.0.0.0 publishes plain http to the network; prefer a reverse proxy with TLS.
# OHS_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1
# Postgres password for the bundled compose database (required by compose, which has no default;
# the quickstart replaces this value with a random one; pass --env-file .env to compose to use it).
# Hex or URL-safe characters only (letters, digits, - . _ ~): compose puts it into OHS_DATABASE_URL as
# it is, where @ / ? # & : % $ or a space would break the URL or change its meaning.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=ohs
# Demo S3 credentials, only used with deploy/docker-compose.s3.yml (pass --env-file .env to compose).
# S3_ACCESS_KEY=ohsaccess
# S3_SECRET_KEY=change-me
# Provider keys, one pair per enabled provider, for example Withings and Oura. Or save them from
# the console (docs/console.md); set here, they win:
# OHS_WITHINGS_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_WITHINGS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_OURA_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_OURA_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Optional: turns Oura webhooks on (see docs/connectors/oura.md).
# OHS_OURA_VERIFICATION_TOKEN=
# Wahoo (see docs/connectors/wahoo.md). The webhook token is optional.
# OHS_WAHOO_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_WAHOO_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_WAHOO_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=
# Sandbox provider, synthetic data for evaluation only (docs/connectors/sandbox.md).
# OHS_SANDBOX_ENABLED=false
# OHS_SANDBOX_SECRET=
# Fitbit through the Google Health API (see docs/connectors/fitbit.md). Webhooks are optional.
# OHS_FITBIT_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_FITBIT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_FITBIT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Garmin Health API (partner program; see docs/connectors/garmin.md). Notifications are refused
# until the inbound token is set: set it and add ?token=<value> to the notification URLs
# registered with Garmin.
# OHS_GARMIN_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_GARMIN_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_GARMIN_INBOUND_TOKEN=
# Eight Sleep (no public API: the account sign-in the Eight Sleep app uses; see
# docs/connectors/eight-sleep.md). On by default with the app's client id and secret as
# published by pyEight; set both only to override them.
# OHS_EIGHT_SLEEP_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_EIGHT_SLEEP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Suunto Cloud API (partner program; see docs/connectors/suunto.md). The notification secret is optional.
# OHS_SUUNTO_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_SUUNTO_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_SUUNTO_SUBSCRIPTION_KEY=
# OHS_SUUNTO_NOTIFICATION_SECRET=
# Ultrahuman Partnership API (see docs/connectors/ultrahuman.md).
# OHS_ULTRAHUMAN_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_ULTRAHUMAN_CLIENT_SECRET=
# intervals.icu (see docs/connectors/intervals-icu.md). The webhook secret is optional.
# OHS_INTERVALS_ICU_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_INTERVALS_ICU_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_INTERVALS_ICU_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# WHOOP (see docs/connectors/whoop.md).
# OHS_WHOOP_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_WHOOP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Polar AccessLink (see docs/connectors/polar.md). The webhook secret is optional.
# OHS_POLAR_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_POLAR_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Strava (see docs/connectors/strava.md). The verify token and subscription id are optional.
# OHS_STRAVA_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_STRAVA_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_STRAVA_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# OHS_STRAVA_SUBSCRIPTION_ID=
# Dexcom API v3 (see docs/connectors/dexcom.md). Region: us (default), eu, jp or sandbox.
# OHS_DEXCOM_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_DEXCOM_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_DEXCOM_REGION=
# OMRON (partner API; see docs/connectors/omron.md). Environment: production (default) or staging.
# OHS_OMRON_CLIENT_ID=
# OHS_OMRON_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OHS_OMRON_ENVIRONMENT=
# Fitbit webhook signature check (see docs/connectors/fitbit.md).
# OHS_FITBIT_WEBHOOK_SIGNATURE=

# Client side only, read by `ohs mcp` on your machine, not by the server.
# Base URL of the instance (default OHS_PUBLIC_URL) and the key it sends.
# OHS_MCP_URL=http://localhost:8080
# OHS_MCP_KEY=
# Read by `ohs import` and `ohs seed` on your machine: the base URL of the instance and, for import, a user token
# with the upload scope (ohs_ut_...).
# OHS_URL=http://localhost:8080
# OHS_TOKEN=
